public static int squarePermutations(int[] A) {
    int n = A.length;
    if (n == 0) return 1;

    int[] sum = new int[n];
    int[][] dp = new int[n][n];

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        sum[i] = A[i] + A[(i + 1) % n];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        dp[i][i] = 1;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            if (isPerfectSquare(sum[i] + sum[j])) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 1] + dp[i + 1][j];
            }
        }
    }

    return dp[0][n - 1];
}

private static boolean isPerfectSquare(int n) {
    int sqrt = (int) Math.sqrt(n);
    return sqrt * sqrt == n;
}
```
Explanation:

We use dynamic programming to solve this problem. We define `dp[i][j]` to be the number of permutations of the subarray `A[i..j]`. We then compute `dp[i][j]` as follows:

1. If `i == j`, then `dp[i][j] = 1`. This is because there is only one permutation of the empty subarray, which is the empty subarray itself.
2. If `i < j`, then `dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-1] + dp[i+1][j]`. This is because we can generate a permutation of the subarray `A[i..j]` by first picking a permutation of the subarray `A[i..j-1]` and then appending the element `A[j]` to the end of it. We can do this in `dp[i][j-1]` ways, and then we can append `A[j]` to each of these permutations in only one way to get a permutation of the subarray `A[i..j]`. We can also do this by first picking a permutation of the subarray `A[i+1..j]` and then prepending the element `A[i]` to the beginning of it. We can do this in `dp[i+1][j]` ways.

To find the number of square permutations, we simply return the number of permutations of the entire array, which is stored in `dp[0][n-1]`.

We define a separate method `isPerfectSquare(int n)` to check if a number is a perfect square. We use the `sqrt()` function to compute the square root of `n`, and then we check if `sqrt(n)` is an integer.

Complexity analysis:

Time complexity: O(n^2), where `n` is the length of the input array `A`.

Space complexity: O(n^2), where `n` is the length of the input array `A`.

